    "math_academy_id": "q-17339",
    "knowledge_point_id": "ff04ceeb-c9f6-5562-b494-204eb62d7f5b",
    "knowledge_point": "Finding the Coordinates of the Points of Intersection of a Trigonometric Curve and a Horizontal Line",
    "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13994895/assets/6839910f0269266caf6888e1e921506bd9585c6805e3d787538771cf379901f7.png)\n\nThe graph shows a portion of the curve $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ and $y=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.$ Which of the following generates the $x$-values of all of their points of intersection? Assume that $n$ is an integer.",
    "worked_solution": "At the intersection points, the $y$-values are the same for both graphs. This gives the equation $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.$ \n\nFirst, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})={30}^{∘}\n$$\n\nOur first solution lies in the $1$st quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}={30}^{∘}.$ Since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x>0,$ the second solution must lie in the fourth quadrant, and it also has ${\\theta }_{R}={30}^{∘}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13994895/assets/8c6e3ae6bdbfb1c66aee719f77225453992fffe6f3c6e3dfdc3f1ff92c59863a.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$  lies in the $4$th quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={360}^{∘}-{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & ={360}^{∘}-{30}^{∘} \\\\  & ={330}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution for the given equation is\n\n$$\nx={30}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘},\\,x={330}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘}.\n$$",
